  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14093769",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-4726",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals",
      "problem": "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that ${\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=18,{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5,$ and ${\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{2}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the adjacent intervals rule and substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the sum rule for definite integrals and substituting the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{7}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 18-10 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x+4 \\\\ 4 & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\int }_{0}^{3}(f(x)-g(x))\\text{d}x=4.$",
