      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Normal Vector To a Plane Given by Parametric Equation",
      "problem": "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 12 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨3,0,4⟩$ and $w=⟨1,-1,1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+j-3k \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨4,1,-3⟩.$",
